From d93e2720a4f7bc9f1f2185f4800be230215fbb5c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adrien Delmas <adrien@adriendelmas.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 09:11:25 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] wetmap: a per-object state kept across frames, the Wet State
 node (S2)

Wet map (addition, docs/WETMAP_PLAN.md). A 'Wet State' shader node reads
the value the previous frame left for its object and outputs
min(max(previous, contact), max wet). On the first wet_accum_samples
samples of the primary camera hits (the AOV idiom) it adds contact, wet
rate, dry time and max wet into per-object device accumulators, with a
count. WetStateManager, owned by the Scene, sizes the arrays to the
objects after the ObjectManager, uploads the previous state, zeroes the
accumulators whenever Session::reset cuts a render short, reads the sums
back on the session thread when a render ran to its end, and - when the
host says the frame changed - folds them into the state:
wet = clamp(wet * exp(-dt / dry_time) + wet_rate * dt * contact, 0, max).
The state is kept by Object::wet_key (the delegate writes the prim path and
the instance index), not by the object index, which moves between frames.
Unseen objects keep their value. Integrator socket wet_accum_samples (16).
OSL form: the current contact only.

Measured with tools/bench_wet.py --gpu (sequence of eight frames under
husk, contact 0.75, rate 50, dry time 0.25 s at 24 fps): CPU 0.751, 1.0,
1.0, 1.0 (the plane gone, the state carried), 0.855, 0.732, 0.627, 0.539;
OptiX 0.749, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0, 0.847, 0.718, 0.608, 0.516 against the expected
0.75, 1, 1, 1, 0.846, 0.717, 0.607, 0.513. Frame 2 rendered twice in one
run reads 0.75 both times: the same frame does not advance the state. The
CPU's drift comes from its AO (0.8 % of the rays stopped by the probe's own
coplanar triangle), not from the state.
